From ef67186afbed4af5793288647564d59c7d69cc29 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vuln Patch Pipeline <vuln-patch@chainguard.dev>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 21:31:28 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Refuse file-source paths into sibling directories that share
 the root's name prefix

AbstractFileSource.assertFilePathIsUnderRoot(String) guards every
FileSource read, write and delete (getBinaryFileNamed, getTextFileNamed,
writeTextFile, writeBinaryFile and deleteFile). It canonicalised the root
and the requested path, which stops plain "../" traversal, but then
compared them with a bare filePath.startsWith(rootPath) and no trailing
separator. A sibling directory whose name merely starts with the root's
name (for example "__files-archive" or "__filesEVIL" next to "__files")
therefore passed the check, so a stub bodyFileName or an admin
PUT /__admin/files/{filename} value resolving into that sibling could
read or overwrite a file outside the configured root (CWE-22, CWE-73).
The check now requires the resolved path to equal the root or to start
with the root followed by File.separator, and throws
NotAuthorisedException otherwise.

ClasspathFileSource, used by usingFilesUnderClasspath(...), had no
containment check at all on its file-system-backed reads:
getBinaryFileNamed and getTextFileNamed opened new File(rootDirectory,
name) directly, so "../secret.txt" or a sibling-prefix name read a file
outside the root. Both now canonicalise that same joined path and apply
the same root-or-root-plus-separator rule, throwing
NotAuthorisedException otherwise or when the path cannot be
canonicalised. Reads from jar, zip, war and ear classpath locations are
unchanged.

Names that resolve inside the root, including nested paths, names with a
leading separator and the root itself, are handled as before.

Adds EditStubFileTaskPathTraversalTest, AdminApiFileTraversalHttpTest and
ClasspathFileSourceContainmentTest.

Cgp-Finding: CGP-29c2-rpxq-q9xm
